('P', 'Posttraumatic stress disorder (PTSD) is a lasting reaction to a traumatic event in which the memory keeps intruding, the person avoids reminders, beliefs about self and world turn negative, and the body stays on alert. In DSM-5-TR it sits in "Trauma- and Stressor-Related Disorders," with acute stress disorder and adjustment disorders (American Psychiatric Association [APA], 2022).'),
('P', '**How common:** across 24 countries, 70.4% of adults had experienced at least one trauma, and rape, other sexual assault, stalking, and the unexpected death of a loved one carried the largest share of the PTSD burden (Kessler et al., 2017). In a U.S. sample, 89.7% reported a DSM-5 traumatic event and lifetime PTSD was 8.3%, higher in women (Kilpatrick et al., 2013).'),
('P', '**Who develops it:** risk factors each have modest effects. Factors during and after the trauma, such as its severity, **lack of social support**, and added life stress, predicted PTSD more strongly than factors before it (Brewin et al., 2000).'),
('P', '**Counselor framing:** most people exposed to trauma do not develop PTSD. For those who do, trauma-focused therapy is the first-line treatment, and a counselor trained in it can do much of the work.'),
('H', '2. Core symptoms to watch for'),
('P', 'DSM-5-TR criteria for adults, paraphrased (APA, 2022); use the manual itself for formal diagnosis.'),
('B', '**Exposure** to actual or threatened death, serious injury, or sexual violence: directly, as a witness, by learning it happened to someone close (if violent or accidental), or by repeated exposure to grim details at work.'),
('B', '**Intrusion** (1 or more): unwanted memories, nightmares, flashbacks, distress at reminders, or strong body reactions to reminders.'),
('B', '**Avoidance** (1 or more) of trauma-related thoughts and feelings or of outside reminders.'),
('B', '**Negative changes in thinking and mood** (2 or more), including exaggerated negative beliefs ("I am bad," "no one can be trusted"), distorted blame of self or others, persistent fear, guilt, or shame, loss of interest, feeling cut off from others, and inability to feel positive emotions.'),
('B', '**Arousal and reactivity** (2 or more): irritability or anger, reckless behavior, hypervigilance, exaggerated startle, poor concentration, and sleep problems.'),
('B', '**Duration and impact:** more than 1 month, with significant distress or impairment, not due to a substance or medical condition. Specifiers: with dissociative symptoms; with delayed expression (full criteria 6 or more months after the event).'),
('P', '**Related diagnoses (APA, 2022):** *acute stress disorder* (3 days to 1 month after the trauma); *adjustment disorder* (a stressor that need not be traumatic, with milder symptoms); *prolonged grief disorder* (see its own review). Children 6 and under have separate criteria.'),
('P', '**Screening tools:** the 5-item PC-PTSD-5 showed excellent accuracy in primary care (AUC 0.94); a cut score of 3 maximized sensitivity (Prins et al., 2016). The 20-item PCL-5 tracks symptoms over time and showed strong reliability (α = .94, test-retest r = .82) (Blevins et al., 2015).'),
('P', '**Suicide risk:** across 50 studies, PTSD was linked to past suicide attempts and to past and current suicidal thinking, partly through co-occurring depression (Krysinska & Lester, 2010). Ask directly.'),
