('B', '**Breadth of evidence:** across 114 trials (8,171 people), trauma-focused CBT and EMDR had clinically important effects; Cognitive Processing Therapy (CPT), Cognitive Therapy, and Prolonged Exposure had the strongest evidence (Lewis et al., 2020). In 64 trials, effects were large (d about 1.0) and fewer than 4 people needed treatment for one to lose the diagnosis (Cusack et al., 2016).'),
('B', '**CPT and exposure:** among 171 women after rape, CPT and prolonged exposure were both highly effective and better than minimal attention (Resick et al., 2002).'),
('B', '**Cognitive therapy for recent PTSD:** at follow-up, 11% still had PTSD after cognitive therapy versus 61% after a self-help booklet (Ehlers et al., 2003).'),
('B', '**Realistic expectations:** in military PTSD, 49% to 70% improved meaningfully with CPT or prolonged exposure, but about two-thirds still met diagnostic criteria afterward (Steenkamp et al., 2015).'),
('P', '**Medication (referral notes).** SSRIs beat placebo, but the effect was small (SMD −0.28); fluoxetine, paroxetine, sertraline, and venlafaxine had support (Hoskins et al., 2021). At long-term follow-up, psychotherapy beat medication (SMD −0.83) (Merz et al., 2019).'),
('P', '**What to avoid:** single-session psychological debriefing right after a trauma did not prevent PTSD, and one trial found higher PTSD risk a year later (OR 2.88) (Rose et al., 2002).'),
('P', '**CBT for PTSD: how it is used.** PTSD persists when the trauma is appraised in excessively negative ways and the memory is poorly processed, so the person feels a *current* threat (Ehlers & Clark, 2000). Treatment therefore:'),
