('P', '**Trauma can wound faith.** In 1,385 veterans, killing others and failing to prevent death weakened religious faith, directly and through guilt, and weakened faith and guilt drove continued use of treatment more than symptom severity did (Fontana & Rosenheck, 2004). *Moral injury*, the lasting harm of perpetrating, failing to prevent, or witnessing acts that violate deeply held moral beliefs, has spiritual as well as psychological effects (Litz et al., 2009).'),
('P', '**Intervention studies (small, but growing):**'),
('B', '**Christian-background women** (randomized, n = 43): an 11-session spiritually focused group for older survivors of abuse lowered depression, anxiety, and PTSD symptoms, with gains kept at 3 months (Bowland et al., 2012).'),
('B', '**Building Spiritual Strength** (veterans): an 8-session spiritually integrated group reduced self-reported PTSD symptoms versus a wait-list (Harris et al., 2011). In a second trial (n = 138), it matched present-centered group therapy on clinician-rated PTSD and did better at easing distress in the relationship with God (Harris et al., 2018).'),
('B', '**Spiritually Integrated CPT:** a 12-session adaptation of CPT that draws on clients’ faith to address moral injury (Pearce et al., 2018). Evidence so far is a 6-person pilot showing improved PTSD symptoms and attachment to God (Duckworth et al., 2026).'),
('P', '**Honest summary for teaching:**'),
('N', 'Trauma-focused CBT (CPT, cognitive therapy, prolonged exposure) is the evidence base. Faith integration supports it; it does not replace it.'),
('N', 'Spiritual struggle and moral injury are common after trauma and predict worse outcomes, so assess them. Positive religious coping and forgiveness predict better outcomes.'),
